    {
      "title": "At least 57 people die as tens of thousands cross to Spanish enclave of Ceuta from Morocco",
      "link": "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2026/jul/31/ceuta-spain-morocco-border-deaths",
      "description": "<p>Pedro Sánchez decries ‘violation of territorial integrity’ as other EU countries criticise Madrid over incident</p><p>At least 57 people have died trying to reach the Spanish enclave of Ceuta after as many as 50,000 migrants entered the tiny territory from Morocco in what Spain’s prime minister, Pedro Sánchez, called “a violation of Spain’s territorial integrity”.</p><p>The huge influx, which took place over the course of just 24 hours, overwhelmed the city of 85,000 people, triggered a political crisis for Madrid and led to a mixed reaction from other EU countries, including calls for Spain to be suspended from the Schengen free-movement zone.</p> <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/world/2026/jul/31/ceuta-spain-morocco-border-deaths\">Continue reading...</a>",
      "source_name": "The Guardian World",
      "published": "2026-07-31T17:52:11"
    },
    {
      "title": "Italy says it’s temporarily suspending EU Schengen arrangement with Spain after 60,000 migrants reach Ceuta – as it happened",
      "link": "https://www.theguardian.com/world/live/2026/jul/31/cueta-spain-morocco-italy-migration-schengen-europe-latest-news-updates",
      "description": "<p>The measure will impact people travelling by plane or boat between the two countries</p><p><strong>Spanish prime minister Pedro Sánchez is now in Ceuta, accompanied by interior minister Fernando Grande-Marlaska.</strong></p><p>He first visited the Tarajal border crossing, and is now talking to officials from local authorities, border and police services.</p> <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/world/live/2026/jul/31/cueta-spain-morocco-italy-migration-schengen-europe-latest-news-updates\">Continue reading...</a>",
      "source_name": "The Guardian World",
      "published": "2026-07-31T17:43:45"
    },
    {
      "title": "After Tens of Thousands of Migrants Enter Spanish Territory, Most Return to Morocco",
      "link": "https://www.nytimes.com/live/2026/07/31/world/spain-ceuta-migrants-morocco",
      "description": "About 48,000 of the up to 60,000 people who came into Ceuta from Morocco returned within a day. Still, the sudden influx created a crisis for Spain’s pro-immigration government. Nearly three dozen died.",
      "source_name": "NYT World News",
      "published": "2026-07-31T22:10:40"
    },
    {
